.TH "SKARN-GUARD" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-guard \- Pre\-execution hook: read an event on stdin, emit a verdict
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBguard\fR [report|accept <fingerprint>] [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Real\-time pre\-execution hook for AI coding assistants; reads an event on
stdin and emits a verdict. Server\-side agent runtimes call it through the sdk
host, which reads Skarn's own versioned envelope and writes one JSON verdict
object. guard report summarizes the audit log and forecasts what enforcing
would cost. It recommends switching the hooks to enforce only once the flagged
calls in the window span five or more days with no deny or would\-be deny
recorded among them. A recorded ask does not stop the recommendation, even on
a host that answers an ask with a block in enforce mode, and neither does a
detect\-only record; the recommendation carries the skarn setup command for
each host setup wires and the manual step for any other host; the sdk host
gets the step that switches its runtime client to enforce. The guard logs
nothing but flagged calls, so a window with none is no evidence that the hook
ran and gets no recommendation. At a terminal it also names, once per release,
a hook in your own per\-user assistant config that has fallen behind this
build. guard accept records a flagged finding as a false positive so the guard
stops flagging it. guard report counts an sdk window by runtime.
.PP
Control the real\-time pre\-execution hook used for editor/agent integration
with Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, the
Antigravity CLI, Kimi Code, Cline, and the Kiro CLI. report reads the guard's
own audit log rather than an event. It summarizes the flagged calls per
assistant (verdicts, top rules, latency, the most recent denies), states what
enforcing that window would have cost in blocks per day and prompts per day,
and prints the enforce flip once the window spans at least five days with zero
denies; for a host wired by skarn setup, that flip is a skarn setup \-\-update
\-\-mode enforce command, which leaves plugin\-installed hooks in audit. That
command flips the user\-scope hooks only, which for Copilot in Code are the
profile hook files, so for every host setup wires at project scope the text
output adds the skarn setup \-\-update \-\-agent <agent> \-\-scope project
\-\-mode enforce run for a project's hook file: under Copilot in Code it names
\-\-agent copilot and a folder project's .github/hooks/skarn.json, the file
Code on macOS was observed running, and for Cline it names a repository's
\&.clinerules/hooks and adds the hand edit for ~/.cline/hooks. Run at a terminal
it reads one thing more, your own per\-user hook configs, so it can name a
hook that has fallen behind this build once per release; piped or as json it
skips those configs, and reads the Copilot in Code hook configuration only to
find the expected Code log, as described below. The guard logs only flagged
calls, so every count and every rate report prints is over flagged actions,
never over all traffic. accept closes that loop. An audit record whose top
finding carries a secret gets a fingerprint, the same secret\-scoped key check
writes to SARIF partialFingerprints, and a value fingerprint that names the
value alone. report prints the accept command with the value fingerprint under
each such deny, and `skarn guard accept <fingerprint>` records the value as a
false positive, so every rule that fires on that value stops flagging it on
any call, check included. A PII finding gets no value fingerprint, so its
accept line carries the rule fingerprint and covers that rule only. A
secret\-less deny \- a structural egress detector, a typosquat, a chain \-
carries no fingerprint and no accept line. Only the highest\-severity finding
of a call carries the fingerprint, so a call hiding several distinct secrets
peels one accept at a time. In the log record, the tool name is screened
first, as \-\-log describes, and a value found in the call is then masked
wherever the record's working directory, session id or a kept tool name copies
it. The command\-line password detector, which reports a password passed to a
database, directory, file\-share or registry client in a shell tool call, and
the structural egress detectors are built into the engine rather than the rule
pack. The egress detectors tier themselves. An egress whose whole payload is
visible in the command itself is held at the ask tier: a bare request, a
literal body, or query parameters, with no file sent, no credential store
named, no earlier secret read in the same call, and no payload bytes the
command text does not show. A command substitution, an environment expansion,
a shell redirect, a form read, or a pipe feeding the target all keep the deny,
and so does a payload carrying a recognized credential file or upload form, a
co\-detected secret, or recon earlier in the same call, with one exception. A
dynamic payload is reported at high rather than critical when its command
names at least one URL, every destination, proxy and connection override it
names (\-x, \-\-proxy, \-\-socks*, \-\-connect\-to, \-\-resolve, \-\-dns\-*,
\-\-interface, a *_proxy variable on the command line) is a loopback literal
(localhost, 127.x.x.x in canonical octets, ::1, 0.0.0.0), every command
substitution in it is a plain local read (printenv, env, cat and the like with
no pipe or nested command), and every stage of the pipeline is a known network
client or a known local filter with no config variable, unix socket, URL file
or unknown command; it is reported at high because a proxy variable inherited
from the environment, an HTTP redirect (\-L is not read as a reason to keep
critical) or a local forwarder can still carry the payload off the machine
with nothing in the command to show it. That finding never disappears, enforce
mode asks on it as on any other high finding, and a formed chain still denies.
A detection rule that stops at its regex work limit on a tool input has not
read the rest of it, so the guard floors that call the way it floors input it
cannot fully classify: ask, deny under \-\-strict, with a reason that names
the work limit when the floor raised the verdict (the truncation reason wins
when both apply), answered through each host's own ask channel or its
documented degradation. A read of a dotenv file larger than the 4 MiB the
guard reads before the call floors the same way on Claude Code, the Copilot
CLI and Copilot in Code when the lines the read asks for are not all inside
those bytes, and on VS Code Copilot Chat a read_file of such a file floors
whatever lines it asks for, with a reason that says the file is larger than
the guard reads; a read the guard loads nothing for (a missing, empty or
binary file, or a path the guard declines to open) keeps its verdict. A call
that removes, downgrades, silences or edits the skarn hook, on a hook file of
any supported assistant, is reported as agent\-hook\-removal: a removal, a
downgrade or a silencing is denied when the hook file the guard reads proves
the effect, and every other such call floors the same way. A shell command
that names a credential file and runs a program whose name is computed at run
time is reported as unresolved\-program\-exfiltration and floors the same way.
Ask means a real approval prompt on the primary shell surfaces (Claude Code
PreToolUse outside bypassPermissions and dontAsk, Copilot preToolUse on both
the CLI and VS Code, Cursor shell and MCP hooks) and a block on the channels
with no ask (Codex, Claude Code PermissionRequest, Copilot permissionRequest,
Cursor file\-read events, Gemini, Grok Build, the Antigravity CLI, Kimi Code,
Cline, the Kiro CLI). Both subverbs are ungated: report reads the log, the
Copilot in Code hook configuration, plus your own hook configs at a terminal,
and accept writes only a file. With no \-\-log and no SKARN_GUARD_LOG, report
lists guard\-copilot\-code\-audit.jsonl when that log exists, and before it
exists when a Code profile hook logs there and the report covers copilot\-code
(no \-\-agent, or \-\-agent copilot\-code), reading the profile hook files and
the layers doctor reads with them to find out; when no expected log exists yet
beside such a profile, the text output names skarn doctor and one flagged Code
action as the next step, not skarn setup \-\-update. On a flagged call the
guard consults the personal accepted\-findings baseline it shares with check
(~/.config/skarn/baseline.json, the file form only, never the org\-union
directory, so the guard can never hit a paid gate). A finding accepted as a
false positive is dropped from the verdict. A finding a human confirmed as a
REAL leak (`skarn baseline audit`, label true\-positive) is never suppressed
and keeps being blocked until the credential is rotated, and a value accept
never silences another rule on that value. A correlated attack chain still
denies even when every secret in the call has been accepted, and a baseline
that is missing, unreadable, or malformed suppresses nothing at all: a broken
baseline means more blocking, never less. Server\-side agent runtimes call the
guard through the sdk host and its versioned envelope.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-guard\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
audit reports only; enforce emits a deny or ask verdict. Enforce needs a
paid\-tier license; an unlicensed guard, or one on the free tier, runs
audit\-only. One of: audit, enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Gate Read operations (credential\-file reconnaissance) on the hosts that do
not gate them by default. Claude Code gates Read without this flag, and so do
the Copilot CLI, Copilot in Code and VS Code Copilot Chat for every
pre\-execution read; on those hosts the guard scans the file the read names
before it runs; the exceptions are a VS Code Copilot Chat view or read, which
names no path field the guard reads a file from and is judged on its arguments
only, and a Copilot post\-tool view, which is still gated only with this flag.
On every host, turn the ask the guard gives an event it cannot fully classify
(unparseable, oversized, a repeated key, a tool input nested past the flatten
bound, or a read of a dotenv file whose requested lines lie past the 4 MiB the
guard reads) into a deny. Without it that event asks in enforce on every host,
degraded to the host's deny where it has no ask channel; a
scan\-infrastructure failure (unreadable rules) is unchanged by this flag on
Claude and the sdk host, which ask, and fails closed only with it on every
other host. On the sdk host the guard scans every tool kind, read included,
without this flag, so \-\-strict changes only the verdict an unclassifiable
call gets there.
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Hook host. By default, auto\-detect from the event shape. Pass codex for Codex
CLI and copilot for Copilot CLI: both send Claude\-shaped events that skarn
cannot tell apart from Claude's. Gemini CLI, Grok Build, the Antigravity CLI,
Kimi Code and Cline each send a payload key no other host sends, so they
auto\-detect too, and the shipped configs pin them anyway. On Kimi Code that
pin is load\-bearing rather than uniform policy: a host embedding kimi's older
hook engine sends no client_type, and its Claude\-shaped prompt event then
fails open under the Claude adapter. On Cline the shipped executables pin
\-\-agent cline all the same, because a hook file copied between machines and
forks carries nothing else that names the host. The Kiro CLI never
auto\-detects: a V3 tool event has the shape of a Claude Code or Copilot CLI
event and a V2 tool event that of a Copilot CLI one, so pass kiro, as every
shipped Kiro entry does; without the pin a Kiro event reaches an adapter that
leaves it unscanned or answers in a shape Kiro ignores, and in enforce an
unpinned V2 tool event is refused as unparseable, which blocks every tool
call. Grok Build is the one host that overrides an explicit pin: an event
delivered by grok's own hook runner is scanned by the grok adapter even where
the invoking config pins another host, because grok runs Claude\-config and
Cursor\-config hooks with its own payload dialect. With \-\-self\-test, all
(the default) tests the claude, cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok,
antigravity, kimi, cline, and kiro adapters, copilot on both its CLI and VS
Code dialects and kiro on both its V2 and V3 harnesses; the self\-test also
tests the sdk adapter. With report, this option restricts the summary to one
assistant, repeatable or comma\-separated, and copilot\-code selects the
records the copilot adapter labels when a Copilot in Code hook runs with a
COPILOT_HOME that names a Code profile. Use sdk for a server\-side agent
runtime that calls the guard through Skarn's own versioned envelope rather
than a vendor hook payload; an envelope with an integer v and a string runtime
auto\-detects after every vendor tell, and your runtime client pins \-\-agent
sdk anyway. One of: claude, cursor, codex, copilot, gemini, grok, antigravity,
kimi, cline, all, sdk, kiro.
.TP
\fB\-\-event\fR \fIname\fR
The hook event this config entry fires on, for a host whose payload does not
name its own event. The Copilot CLI needs it: its permissionRequest payload
carries a subset of preToolUse's documented fields and nothing separates the
two, so a permissionRequest entry must say so or the guard reads it as a
preToolUse and answers in a schema that event does not understand. Default:
the event is inferred from the payload shape, which never yields
permissionRequest. Codex names its own event and the pin never overrides that
name; skarn reads the pin only where the name is missing, so the deny for a
payload that never parsed, or a call carrying no hook_event_name, takes the
pinned shape instead of the PreToolUse default, with or without \-\-strict.
Claude honors the pin the same way for a payload that never parsed: a
permissionRequest pin answers with the PermissionRequest behavior deny, and
with no pin the PreToolUse ask stands, which another event may ignore. Ignored
by every other host, and by the VS Code Copilot dialect. One of: preToolUse,
permissionRequest.
.TP
\fB\-\-self\-test\fR
Verify the guard's own code path instead of reading an event. Skarn feeds
synthetic fixture events through each adapter's real code path, then reports
the verdicts, latency, and a guard\-log write probe. Every adapter gets three:
one benign, one carrying a fake credential, and one shell call that echoes the
base64 encoding of a fake AWS secret key, which passes only when the
aws\-secret\-access\-key rule denies it from the decoded payload. Every
adapter also gets a deep fixture, a tool input nested past the guard's flatten
bound, which passes only when the adapter marks it unclassifiable and gives it
the verdict every unclassifiable call gets (ask, deny under \-\-strict),
printed on the line as deep nesting ask, deep nesting deny, or deep nesting
would\-ask in audit. Every adapter whose host hands the hook the tool output
gets a fourth, a post\-tool event whose output carries a fake GCP
service\-account key and a fake AWS secret key, which passes only when the
gcp\-service\-account\-key rule denies it from the tool result and the
aws\-secret\-access\-key rule denies the AWS key as found in the tool result
itself: claude, codex, copilot on both dialects, gemini, grok, and cline. The
cursor output fixture is a beforeReadFile event, which the guard gates only
under \-\-strict, so it runs only with \-\-strict. Kimi and antigravity carry
no output fixture: the kimi adapter gates no post\-tool event, and an
antigravity PostToolUse carries no tool output. Each expected rule must deny
on its own and from the named source, so a deny produced by another rule, a
dead input decode, an adapter that drops the tool output, or a dead
tool\-result scan fails the line. The kimi fixtures carry kimi's own
client_type stamp and content\-block prompt shape, so they prove the routing
the shipped pin also asserts. The mode in the header is this invocation's,
from \-\-guard\-mode and the license; it is never read from a host config, so
a bare run reports audit whatever skarn setup wired, and skarn doctor is where
the wired mode is reported. The fake credential is only ever printed redacted,
and the self\-test deliberately ignores the accepted\-findings baseline, so an
accepted fingerprint can never make a dead hook look alive. What it proves is
the binary, the adapter and the log write. It runs entirely in this process
and reads no host config, so it never proves that a host is calling the hook:
skarn doctor reads the host configs for that, and on a host doctor does not
cover (kimi today) the only proof is a flagged action driven through the host
itself. Exit 0 when every adapter passes, 1 otherwise; works unlicensed, with
audit semantics. The sdk adapter gets the benign, credential, encoded, deep
and output fixtures in its own envelope. Its output fixture is a post_tool_use
whose result string carries the fake keys.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
On the hook invocation, `skarn guard \-\-log <path|none>` names where this
call appends its redacted JSONL record. A path takes precedence over
$SKARN_GUARD_LOG; none writes no record even when that variable is set; no
flag at all falls back to the variable. Skarn refuses a path that expands to a
parent\-directory segment or carries a control character: no record is written
and there is no fallback destination, because a fallback would derive from
$XDG_CACHE_HOME or $HOME, which the same hook command can also set. With
report: a guard log to summarize, repeatable. Default: $SKARN_GUARD_LOG when
set, otherwise the log skarn setup wires for each assistant
($XDG_CACHE_HOME/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl, plus the home cache beside
it when a log already exists there). A log that does not exist is named in the
output with the command that wires it, never silently counted as zero, and a
configured path the guard refuses is named as refused rather than shown as
empty. A record the sdk adapter writes also carries the envelope runtime label
and, when the envelope names one, the identity it asserts, which the guard
does not authenticate. A runtime label longer than 64 bytes or outside
[A\-Za\-z0\-9._\-], an identity longer than 128 bytes or outside
[A\-Za\-z0\-9._@+\-], and either one shaped like a credential, read as one by
the detection rules, whose own scan stopped a detection rule at its regex work
limit, or containing a value the call's own scan matched is recorded as
withheld; an email address in identity is kept. On every host, a record's tool
name that contains a value the call's own scan found, that carries a
credential\-shaped piece (16 or more mixed letters and digits, read across
underscores and hyphens up to an all\-lowercase word, or a long value after a
credential word), or that is an MCP tool name on a call whose scan decoded a
secret, is written as mcp__redacted; the sdk adapter writes sdk\-tool instead,
and also for an empty name, a name over 64 bytes, one outside letters, digits,
dot, underscore and hyphen, or one that reads as credential\-shaped as a
whole. Any other tool name is written unchanged, so a credential under 16
bytes, or one an all\-lowercase piece breaks up, can reach the log when the
call's scan did not detect it. With report and no \-\-log, the sdk log
(guard\-sdk\-audit.jsonl under the same cache) is listed only when it exists
or when \-\-agent sdk names it, because no laptop host writes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-window\fR \fIspan\fR
With report: how far back to summarize, as <N>d or all. The default is 7d.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With report: text renders the summary, the enforce\-cost forecast, and the
recommendation. json emits the whole aggregate as one object with stable field
names for fleet scripts, including a forecast object and the denominator
string that names what its rates are rates of. One of: text, json. The default
is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIfile\fR
With accept: the accepted\-findings file to write. Defaults to the personal
baseline the guard itself reads (~/.config/skarn/baseline.json, honoring
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME), created on first use. `skarn baseline accept` always names
its file instead, because an accept the guard does not read would not stop the
block. Always a file, never a directory: the org\-union form is not reachable
from the guard. Accepting a fingerprint already recorded as a confirmed true
positive, or a value fingerprint whose value a true positive confirms, is
refused, so a fingerprint\-only accept can never re\-silence a real leak.
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
With accept: why this finding is a false positive, recorded in the baseline
entry. Do not paste a secret here; the text is stored verbatim.
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
With accept: override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the entry; defaults
to $SKARN_IDENTITY.
.SH "HOOK EVENTS"
Every hook event skarn guard handles, one table per host. Fires on is what
triggers the event. Enforce is what enforce mode does with a finding: deny or
ask, deny (a would\-be ask becomes a deny), cancel task (the whole task ends),
advises only, or log only (the host gives the hook no way to block, so only
the guard log records the verdict). Shipped says whether the integration
configs wire the event or leave it opt\-in. Audit mode never blocks on a
finding; the line above each table says how it speaks and how a guard failure
lands.
.SS "Claude Code"
Audit mode advises without deciding, so Claude Code's own permission dialog
can still offer to save a flagged command, secret included, as a permission
rule. A guard crash or timeout fails open on Claude Code unless the hook entry
sets onFailure to block (on Windows without Git Bash a failed shell\-form
PreToolUse or PermissionRequest command hook blocks), and an event the guard
cannot classify asks in enforce (denies under \-\-strict). On PreToolUse in
bypassPermissions or dontAsk every ask is a deny, because Claude Code runs a
hook's ask there without a prompt; an oversized or malformed event whose mode
the guard cannot read keeps the ask unless \-\-strict denies it.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&PermissionRequest	\&permission check	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptExpansion	\&expanded command	\&deny	\&yes
\&TaskCreated	\&new task	\&deny	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
\&PostToolBatch	\&tool result batch	\&deny	\&yes
\&Stop	\&response end	\&advises only	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Cursor"
Audit mode is silent. beforeReadFile is gated only under \-\-strict, which the
shipped config passes on that event alone. In enforce an unparseable event
exits 2 and an input the guard cannot fully classify asks (denies under
\-\-strict), except on preToolUse, where it denies.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&beforeShellExecution	\&shell command	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&beforeReadFile	\&file read	\&deny	\&yes
\&beforeMCPExecution	\&MCP tool call	\&deny or ask	\&yes
\&beforeSubmitPrompt	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&preToolUse	\&file write or delete	\&deny	\&yes
.TE
.SS "Codex CLI"
Codex shares Claude's event names, so pass \-\-agent codex. Audit mode advises
without deciding. In enforce an event the guard cannot classify is denied in
that event's own schema.
.PP
.TS
lB lB lB lB
lB l l l.
Event	Fires on	Enforce	Shipped
\&PreToolUse	\&tool call	\&deny	\&yes
\&PermissionRequest	\&permission check	\&deny	\&yes
\&UserPromptSubmit	\&submitted prompt	\&deny	\&yes
\&PostToolUse	\&tool result	\&deny	\&opt\-in
\&Stop	\&turn end	\&advises only	\&yes
.TE
.SS "GitHub Copilot CLI"
Audit mode advises on preToolUse and postToolUse and is silent on the rest. A
hook loading after Skarn can replace a permissionRequest deny with allow, so
preToolUse is the durable gate. preToolUse fails closed on a non\-zero exit
and open on a timeout. In enforce an unparseable event exits 2 and an input
the guard cannot fully classify asks.
.PP
.TS
